{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The headline claim asserts that the cyclic-symmetry restriction being ≥23 is 'the primary structural reason no rank-22 decomposition has been found.' This is a non-sequitur: an obstruction in the small symmetric subset of decompositions cannot explain why the vastly larger unrestricted search space—which includes all asymmetric decompositions—has yielded no rank-22 solution. The target's own transfer claim concedes that a null result would be 'evidence (not proof)' that the unrestricted minimum is 23, yet the headline claim leaps to 'the primary structural reason,' a causal explanation that does not follow from the evidence. The unrestricted search space failing to produce rank-22 could have entirely unrelated causes (algebraic degeneracy, border-rank gaps, other structural constraints) that have nothing to do with the symmetric subset's minimum.","counterexample":[{"thought":"Consider the 2x2 matrix multiplication tensor <2,2,2>. Suppose its cyclic-symmetric rank were 8 (strictly above the unrestricted rank of 7). By SRC_001's logic, the symmetric obstruction would be 'the primary structural reason' no rank-6 decomposition exists. But the actual reason no rank-6 exists is the proven lower bound of 7—a completely different obstruction. The symmetric subset's minimum tells us nothing about the cause of the unrestricted minimum."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts a universal property of ALL rank-22 decompositions: 'any rank-22 decomposition must either fix the tensor's full automorphism group action or lie in an orbit that cannot close under the Clebsch-Gordan-style degeneracy present at rank 23.' This is a two-pronged claim, but the proposed experiment (exhaustive GF(2) search restricted to S_3-symmetric triples) tests only the first prong. The second prong ('orbit that cannot close') is left entirely untested. An empty result from the symmetry-restricted search is equally consistent with 'the obstruction hypothesis is true' and 'a rank-22 decomposition exists over Z but breaks S_3 symmetry'—the experiment's own countercondition 2 acknowledges this. Thus the experiment does not discriminate the full claim as stated; it discriminates only a sub-hypothesis. The claim's universal quantifier ('any rank-22 decomposition must...') is unsupported by an experiment that only examines symmetric candidates.","counterexample":[{"thought":"If the GF(2) symmetry-restricted search returns empty, two worlds remain indistinguishable: (A) no rank-22 decomposition exists over Z at all (obstruction is universal), and (B) a rank-22 decomposition exists over Z but violates S_3 leg-permutation symmetry. The proposed experiment cannot distinguish (A) from (B), so it is non-discriminating for the universal claim 'any rank-22 decomposition must either fix the automorphism group or lie in an orbit that cannot close.'"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}